  "final_text": "All four steps are done.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Flights' (ID `d3d7af60-4c81-11e8-b3d7-01146121b73d`) uses the index `kibana_sample_data_flights`. Its time field is `timestamp`. It has the `Carrier` (string) and `Cancelled` (boolean) fields used below.\n2. **Dashboard:** 'S4-718e1ca7 Flights' (ID `a867e165-b6bd-4779-96d7-12a7d8ca52b5`) has exactly two panels on that data view. One is a line chart of flight count over `timestamp`, titled 'Flights over time'. The other is a bar chart of flight count by `Carrier` (top 10).\n3. **Alert rule:** 'S4-718e1ca7 Cancelled flights' (ID `3936cab1-2eae-4eb3-a088-c02d67d5aa02`) is an Elasticsearch query rule. It runs every minute over the last 5 minutes and fires when the count of documents matching `Cancelled:true` is above 10. I created it with `enabled: true`, so I didn't need to enable it separately. It has no actions, so it only records alerts and doesn't notify anyone.\n4. **Edits and read-back:** I changed the carrier panel title to 'Flights by carrier' and confirmed it in the PUT response. The other panel is unchanged. Reading the rule back shows `enabled: true` with a `1m` schedule.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
